    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Josh Naylor** as our clear offensive cornerstone. Facing an exploitable arm in Gibson (5.74 xERA), **Josh Naylor** projects at a dominant 78.46 score, bolstered by a significant park factor and a top-three lineup placement. **Cole Young** shares this high-ceiling environment against the same favorable pitching matchup, providing efficient volume out of the leadoff spot for a 63.25 score. While our heavy hitters are primed, we are closely monitoring the bottom of the order where **Brice Turang** earns a strong start (59.45) despite facing the southpaw Springs; his elite speed and high-leverage utility status in our build keep him ahead of players with better platoon splits but lower offensive ceilings.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today's card involved **James Wood**, who sits at a 53.03 score. Despite the daunting matchup against Logan Webb, **James Wood** remains a must-start due to his elite 0.402 xwOBA; he is the most penalized player by the \"Elite Catcher\" defensive metric, but his raw talent outweighs the marginal projection dip. Conversely, we\u2019ve sidelined the rest of our depth, including the inactive **Corbin Carroll**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Austin Riley**, and the injured **Mickey Moniak**, all of whom are unavailable due to schedule gaps or IL status. **Jose Caballero** represents our highest risk today; while he is a critical component of our stolen base strategy, his score of 41.91 is suppressed by the combination of a difficult defensive catcher and a tough matchup against Williams. We are keeping a sharp eye on late-breaking lineup news for **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Henry Bolte**, and **Max Muncy**, as any shift downward in the batting order against the lefty Harrison could force a further reassessment of their fringe-tier scores.",
